        {
          "type": "code",
          "language": "json",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "{\n \"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\",\n \"name\": \"Boolean.lean\",\n \"url\": \"https://uuidna.com/lean/Boolean.lean\",\n \"inLanguage\": \"lean4\",\n \"variableMeasured\": {\n  \"@type\": \"PropertyValue\",\n  \"name\": \"theorems\",\n  \"value\": 6,\n  \"unitText\": \"theorem\"\n }\n}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-sixteen_binary_functions",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THERE ARE EXACTLY SIXTEEN two-input boolean functions, and they are the sixteen values of a nibble: each function IS its four-row truth table, so 2^(2^2) = 16 counts them and no argument is needed beyond the encoding. All sixteen are distinct."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "((List.range 16).map rowsOf).eraseDups.length = 16 ∧ ((2:Nat)^(2^2) = 16)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem sixteen_binary_functions : ((List.range 16).map rowsOf).eraseDups.length = 16 ∧ ((2:Nat)^(2^2) = 16) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-gates_name_their_tables",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE CLASSICAL GATES ARE PARTICULAR ROWS: AND is 0001, OR is 0111, XOR is 0110, NAND is 1110 and NOR is 1000, reading the table over (0,0), (0,1), (1,0), (1,1). Each named gate is one of the sixteen and the line identifies which, so the names are anchored to the enumeration rather than asserted beside it."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(rowsOf 8 = [0,0,0,1]) ∧ (rowsOf 14 = [0,1,1,1]) ∧ (rowsOf 6 = [0,1,1,0]) ∧ (rowsOf 7 = [1,1,1,0]) ∧ (rowsOf 1 = [1,0,0,0])"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem gates_name_their_tables : (rowsOf 8 = [0,0,0,1]) ∧ (rowsOf 14 = [0,1,1,1]) ∧ (rowsOf 6 = [0,1,1,0]) ∧ (rowsOf 7 = [1,1,1,0]) ∧ (rowsOf 1 = [1,0,0,0]) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-nand_rebuilds_the_others",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "NAND IS FUNCTIONALLY COMPLETE, decided rather than claimed: NOT is NAND of a value with itself, AND is the negation of NAND, and OR is NAND of the two negations. Every input pair checked, so the completeness argument is carried out rather than cited — this is why a chip can be one repeated gate."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "((List.range 2).all (fun a => nandB a a == notB a)) ∧ ((List.range 2).all (fun a => (List.range 2).all (fun b => notB (nandB a b) == andB a b))) ∧ ((List.range 2).all (fun a => (List.range 2).all (fun b => nandB (notB a) (notB b) == orB a b)))"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem nand_rebuilds_the_others : ((List.range 2).all (fun a => nandB a a == notB a)) ∧ ((List.range 2).all (fun a => (List.range 2).all (fun b => notB (nandB a b) == andB a b))) ∧ ((List.range 2).all (fun a => (List.range 2).all (fun b => nandB (notB a) (notB b) == orB a b))) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-two_functions_ignore_input",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "EXACTLY TWO OF THE SIXTEEN ARE CONSTANT — the always-false 0000 and the always-true 1111 — so fourteen actually depend on their inputs. Two, and the line proves the count rather than the reader noticing it."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(((List.range 16).filter (fun m => (rowsOf m).eraseDups.length == 1)).length = 2) ∧ (((List.range 16).filter (fun m => (rowsOf m).eraseDups.length > 1)).length = 14)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem two_functions_ignore_input : (((List.range 16).filter (fun m => (rowsOf m).eraseDups.length == 1)).length = 2) ∧ (((List.range 16).filter (fun m => (rowsOf m).eraseDups.length > 1)).length = 14) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-xor_differs_from_or",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "XOR IS NOT OR, and the difference is the single row where both inputs hold: 0110 against 0111. The two agree on three of four rows, which is why the distinction is worth deciding rather than assuming — a gate that agreed everywhere would be the same gate."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(rowsOf 6 ≠ rowsOf 14) ∧ ((rowsOf 6).take 3 = (rowsOf 14).take 3) ∧ (rowsOf 6 = [0,1,1,0])"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem xor_differs_from_or : (rowsOf 6 ≠ rowsOf 14) ∧ ((rowsOf 6).take 3 = (rowsOf 14).take 3) ∧ (rowsOf 6 = [0,1,1,0]) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-implication_is_a_gate",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "IMPLICATION IS ONE OF THE SIXTEEN. Its converse and both negations are also among the sixteen, so the whole of two-input logic is inside the enumeration with nothing left outside it."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(rowsOf 13 = [1,0,1,1]) ∧ (rowsOf 11 = [1,1,0,1]) ∧ (rowsOf 2 = [0,1,0,0])"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem implication_is_a_gate : (rowsOf 13 = [1,0,1,1]) ∧ (rowsOf 11 = [1,1,0,1]) ∧ (rowsOf 2 = [0,1,0,0]) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
